EXT. HOT-AIR BALLOON - THREE THOUSAND FEET - DAWN

A wicker basket the size of a small closet swings under a great striped envelope of orange and teal. Fields below are a quilt of green. The burner roars, rests, roars again.

PERPETUA VANCE-OLUWOLE, 50s, pilot, leather cap and a scarf the length of a river, works the valve and looks at no one. Beside her, ARABELLA HOLT, 40s, passenger in a sequined jacket, clutches the rim with white knuckles. At the far side MURDOCH ABERNETHY, 60s, her father-in-law, dozes upright against a sandbag, and FITZ, 20s, deckhand, practically a vine, clings to a rigging line with a grin that will not stay on.

PERPETUA
(to the basket, all of them)
We're at altitude, folks. Enjoy the view.

Arabella lets out a very small scream. Fitz applauds.

MURDOCH
(waking)
Is it over?

ARABELLA
No, Murdoch.

MURDOCH
Then why am I awake?

PERPETUA
(to herself)
Three thousand feet, two idiots, and a prayer.

Arabella glares at her. Perpetua fires the burner with a cheerful roar.

ARABELLA
Excuse me?

PERPETUA
Nothing.

FITZ
(to Murdoch)
You can see your house from here!

MURDOCH
I sold my house.

FITZ
You can see the new owner's house!

Murdoch peers over the rim. Squints. Nods slowly.

MURDOCH
He mowed the lawn badly.

ARABELLA
(to everyone, brittle)
Can we land now? Please?

PERPETUA
Landing's a negotiation, dear.

ARABELLA
With whom?

PERPETUA
The wind.

A gust shoves the basket sideways. Arabella shrieks and drops to her knees. Murdoch grabs her sequined shoulder, delighted. Fitz whoops. Perpetua, unbothered, adjusts the valve.
